{"abstract":"The DCR Workbench is an online tool for simulation and analysis of collaborative distributed processes specified as DCR graphs. The Workbench is a robust and comprehensive implementation of DCR graphs, providing concrete syntax, specification by refinement, visualisation, simulation, static analysis, time analysis, enforcement, declarative subprocesses, data dependencies, translation to other declarative models, and more. This chapter introduces the Workbench and, through the features of the Workbench, surveys the DCR formalism. The Workbench is available on-line at http: //dcr.tools.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Debois, Søren","Hildebrandt, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383270"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Collaborative distributed processes","DCR Graphs","DCR Workbench","Data dependencies","Declarative subprocesses","Formal specification","Simulation and analysis","Static analysis","Time analysis","Visualisation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the dcr workbench declarative choreographies for collaborative processes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.riverpublishers.com/pdf/ebook/chapter/RP_9788793519817C5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383270","identifier":[{"id":"85053613034","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-88834914-a51b-49fb-ae54-c06a45d5d2e5"],"keyword":["Collaborative distributed processes","DCR Workbench","DCR graphs","Data dependencies","Declarative subprocesses","Formal specification","Simulation and analysis","Static analysis","Time analysis","Visualisation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-0A6905B80B7FA3B5A431983E819FB9E9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.riverpublishers.com/pdf/ebook/chapter/RP_9788793519817C5.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"deboispau","last":"Debois","name":"Debois, Søren","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Simon","key":"gaypedh","last":"Gay","name":"Gay, Simon","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"António","key":"ravarapedh","last":"Ravara","name":"Ravara, António","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788793519824"],"issn":["00010782"],"pages":"99-124","publisher":"River Publishers","series":"River Publishers Series in Automation, Control and Robotics","status":"p","title":"Behavioural Types: from Theory to Tools"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85053613034"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-88834914-a51b-49fb-ae54-c06a45d5d2e5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The DCR Workbench: Declarative Choreographies for Collaborative Processes","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383270","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"(a)Situation faced: Exformatics, a Danish adaptive case-management vendor, wanted to leverage declarative process tools to support the flexible processes found at BRFkredit. However, switching from the more common flow-based notations to a declarative notation brought new challenges in terms of understandability. We undertook the project described in this chapter to investigate and address these challenges. (b)Action taken: We started our investigation by having several full-day and half-day meetings to discuss BRFkredit’s requirements. Based on these requirements, we proposed and developed a prototype hybrid process-modelling approach with which models are defined declaratively, but the possible behavior of the model can be viewed and investigated using flow-based notions. The prototype was then presented to BRFkredit for feedback. (c)Results achieved: Our investigation helped to clarify the requirements for making declarative process models understandable to end users at BRFkredit and showed how a hybrid approach could be used to satisfy these requirements. Based on these insights, we developed tools to enhance our existing declarative modelling framework with flow-based visualizations. (d)Lessons learned: Different stakeholders have different needs and preferred levels of abstraction when process models are used as tools for communication. However, one model that seems to fit most situations is a simple no-branches sequential swimlane diagram that was extracted automatically from a more detailed declarative model. These observations enabled Exformatics to enhance its declarative modelling framework to make it more attractive to end-users.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Debois, Søren","Hildebrandt, Thomas","Marquard, Morten","Slaats, Tijs"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-038329"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adaptive Case Management","Declarative process models","Flow based visualizations","Hybrid process modelling","User requirements in process modelling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hybrid process technologies in the financial sector the case of brfkredit","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82442971/final_published.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-038329","identifier":[{"id":"85041698110","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-765aee03-c92a-447e-9707-f20e4db7fbfe","ku-46063302-61f0-44e0-9061-917eab77de39"],"keyword":["Adaptive case management","Declarative process models","Flow-based visualizations","Hybrid process-modelling","User requirements in process modelling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-11EE05BA16DAB980AD82ECDC70E2AF9B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82442971/final_published.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Software, Data, People & Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"deboispau","last":"Debois","name":"Debois, Søren","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Morten","key":"marquardpau","last":"Marquard","name":"Marquard, Morten","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Tijs","key":"slaatspau","last":"Slaats","locid":"85f00008-e9c6-439c-aae5-e5bd725f2099","name":"Slaats, Tijs","orcid":"0000-0001-6244-6970","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-58307-5_21","eisbn":["9783319583075"],"isbn":["9783319583068"],"issn":["2192810X"],"pages":"397-412","publisher":"Springer","series":"Management for Professionals","status":"p","subtitle":"Digital Innovation and Business Transformation in Practice","title":"Business Process Management Cases"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85041698110"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-765aee03-c92a-447e-9707-f20e4db7fbfe","ku-46063302-61f0-44e0-9061-917eab77de39"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hybrid Process Technologies in the Financial Sector : The Case of BRFkredit","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-038329","year":"2017","yearsub":2019}
{"abstract":"We present the first session typing system guaranteeing request-response liveness properties for possibly non-terminating communicating processes. The types augment the branch and select types of the standard binary session types with a set of required responses, indicating that whenever a particular label is selected, a set of other labels, its responses, must eventually also be selected. We prove that these extended types are strictly more expressive than standard session types. We provide a type system for a process calculus similar to a subset of collaborative BPMN processes with internal (data-based) and external (event-based) branching, message passing, bounded and unbounded looping. We prove that this type system is sound, i.e., it guarantees request-response liveness for dead-lock free processes. We exemplify the use of the calculus and type system on a concrete example of an infinite state system.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Debois, Søren","Hildebrandt, Thomas","Slaats, Tijs","Yoshida, Nobuko"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384582"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Imperial College London"],"keyword":["Bounded recursion","Business processes","Liveness","Process algebra","Session types","Typing system"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Exformatics"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"type checking liveness for collaborative processes with bounded and unbounded recursion","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-12(1:1)2016","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384582","identifier":[{"id":"84960154263","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e12b68e9-0f86-4eb2-b756-d04c028ce72b"],"keyword":["Bounded recursion","Business processes","Liveness","Process algebra","Session types","Typing system"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-9486547B25609DB604C1BC2D3C25C59A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-12(1:1)2016","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.06658v2.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Exformatics"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"imperial college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Imperial College London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"deboispau","last":"Debois","name":"Debois, Søren","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tijs","key":"slaatspau","last":"Slaats","name":"Slaats, Tijs","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nobuko","key":"yoshidapau","last":"Yoshida","name":"Yoshida, Nobuko","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2168/lmcs-12(1:1)2016","issn":["18605974"],"issue":"1","journal":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","pages":"1-38","publisher":"International Federation for Computational Logic","status":"p","title":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84960154263"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e12b68e9-0f86-4eb2-b756-d04c028ce72b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Type-checking Liveness for Collaborative Processes with Bounded and Unbounded Recursion","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384582","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"We present a system model, an enforcement mechanism, and a policy language for the proactive enforcement of timed provisions and obligations. Our approach improves upon existing formalisms in two ways: (1) we exploit the target system's existing functionality to avert policy violations proactively, rather than compensate for them reactively, and, (2) instead of requiring the manual specification of remedial actions in the policy, we automatically deduce required actions directly from the policy. As a policy language, we employ timed dynamic condition response (DCR) processes. DCR primitives declaratively express timed provisions and obligations as causal relationships between events, and DCR states explicitly represent pending obligations. As key technical results, we show that enforceability of DCR policies is decidable, we give a sufficient polynomial time verifiable condition for a policy to be enforceable, and we give an algorithm for determining from a DCR state a sequence of actions that discharge impending obligations.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Basin, David","Debois, Søren","Hildebrandt, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382162"],"event":{"end":"2016-07-01","place":"Lisbon, pt","start":"2016-06-27","title":"IEEE Computer Security Foundations Symposium","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Swiss Federal Institute of Technology Zürich"],"keyword":["Causal Relationships","DCR Processes","Policy Language","Proactive Enforcement","Timing Provisions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Exformatics"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"in the nick of time proactive prevention of obligation violations","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82372867/csf16_obligations.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382162","identifier":[{"id":"84985916954","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3cb8caac-af63-4d69-9197-794e559e2b44"],"keyword":["Causal Relationships","DCR Processes","Policy Language","Proactive Enforcement","Timing Provisions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-372B39191641CC66749A362BCEA0A17E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82372867/csf16_obligations.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"swiss federal institute of technology zürich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Swiss Federal Institute of Technology Zürich","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Exformatics"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"David","key":"basinpau","last":"Basin","name":"Basin, David","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"deboispau","last":"Debois","name":"Debois, Søren","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/csf.2016.16","issn":["19401434"],"journal":"IEEE Computer Security Foundations Symposium. Proceedings","pages":"120-134","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Computer Security Foundations Symposium. Proceedings","title_org":"I E E E Computer Security Foundations Symposium. Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84985916954"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3cb8caac-af63-4d69-9197-794e559e2b44"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"In the Nick of Time: Proactive Prevention of Obligation Violations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382162","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
